Body:
+1'ing with a concrete pain point: **tmux's `monitor-bell` window flag** (`!` in the status line) is the main reason BEL matters. The built-in `attention.sound` plays an audio file through system audio — it never writes `\x07` to the PTY, so tmux/screen never light up. The result: in a multiplexed session the attention signal is invisible unless you're actively listening, even with `attention.enabled: true`.

For anyone hitting this today, here's a working `TuiPlugin` on 1.17.x. The snippet in the OP is slightly stale — `TuiPlugin` now returns `Promise<void>` (not `Hooks`), and disposal goes through `api.lifecycle.onDispose`:

```ts
// ~/.config/opencode/plugins/terminal-bell.ts
import type { TuiPlugin } from "@opencode-ai/plugin/tui";

const RING_EVENTS = [
  "session.idle",
  "question.asked",
  "question.v2.asked",
  "permission.asked",
  "permission.v2.asked",
  "session.error",
] as const;

let last = 0;
const ring = () => {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (now - last < 1000) return; // coalesce the v1/v2 event pairs
  last = now;
  try { process.stdout.write("\x07"); } catch { /* never throw from a hook */ }
};

export const tui: TuiPlugin = async (api) => {
  const off = RING_EVENTS.map((t) => api.event.on(t, ring));
  api.lifecycle.onDispose(() => off.forEach((u) => u()));
};
```

Still think this belongs built-in. An `attention.terminal_bell: true` option that writes `\x07` alongside the sound (mirroring `TuiAttentionSoundName` — `done`, `question`, `permission`, `error`, `subagent_done`) would cover every multiplexer user without forcing them to ship a plugin, and the `TuiAttention` API already centralizes the "when to ring" logic.
